/*
Problem: Subtree of Another Tree
Platform: LeetCode (LC 572)
Topic: Binary Tree / DFS / Recursion

---------------------------------------------------------

Approach:

We are given:

1. root
   → Main tree

2. subRoot
   → Tree that may be a subtree of root

A subtree means:

Choose any node in root,
and the entire tree starting from that
node must be identical to subRoot.

---------------------------------------------------------

Idea:

For every node in root:

Check:

"Is the tree rooted here exactly the
same as subRoot?"

If yes:

Return true.

Otherwise:

Repeat the same process for:

• Left subtree

• Right subtree

---------------------------------------------------------

Helper Function:

isSameTree(p, q)

Purpose:

Checks whether two trees are
completely identical.

Conditions:

1. Both NULL

Return true.

2. One NULL

Return false.

3. Values must match.

4. Left subtrees must match.

5. Right subtrees must match.

---------------------------------------------------------

Main Algorithm:

At every node of root:

Step 1:

If

root->val == subRoot->val

then call

isSameTree(root, subRoot)

If true,

Return true.

--------------------------------

Step 2:

Otherwise search recursively in:

root->left

or

root->right

If either returns true,

subRoot exists.

---------------------------------------------------------

Example:

root:

        3
       / \
      4   5
     / \
    1   2

subRoot:

      4
     / \
    1   2

--------------------------------

Visit:

3

Not equal.

Search left.

--------------------------------

Visit:

4

Values match.

Call:

isSameTree()

Compare:

4 == 4 ✔

1 == 1 ✔

2 == 2 ✔

Entire tree matches.

Return true.

---------------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

Every node in root is treated as
a possible starting point.

Whenever values match,
we verify the entire subtree.

If none matches,
the answer is false.

---------------------------------------------------------

Time Complexity:

Let:

n = nodes in root

m = nodes in subRoot

Worst case:

O(n × m)

Because at many nodes,
isSameTree() may compare up to m nodes.

---------------------------------------------------------

Space Complexity:

O(h)

h = height of the tree

Recursive call stack.

Worst case:

O(n)

Balanced tree:

O(log n)

---------------------------------------------------------
*/

class Solution {
public:

    // Check whether two trees are identical
    bool isSameTree(TreeNode* p,
                    TreeNode* q) {

        if (p == NULL || q == NULL)
            return p == q;

        bool isLeftSame =
            isSameTree(p->left, q->left);

        bool isRightSame =
            isSameTree(p->right, q->right);

        return isLeftSame &&
               isRightSame &&
               p->val == q->val;
    }

    bool isSubtree(TreeNode* root,
                   TreeNode* subRoot) {

        // Base case
        if (root == NULL || subRoot == NULL)
            return root == subRoot;

        // Check if subtree starts here
        if (root->val == subRoot->val &&
            isSameTree(root, subRoot))
            return true;

        // Search in left or right subtree
        return isSubtree(root->left, subRoot) ||
               isSubtree(root->right, subRoot);
    }
};
